{"subjectId":"c","id":"arrays","title":"Arrays","group":"Arrays & Strings","order":18,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An array is a fixed-size block of <em>contiguous</em> elements of one type, indexed from 0. C never checks the index — staying in bounds is your job.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Declaring and initialising</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n    int b[5] = {1, 2};              /* rest are zero: 1 2 0 0 0 */\n    int c[] = {7, 8, 9};            /* size deduced: 3 */\n    int d[5] = {0};                 /* all zeros */\n    int e[6] = {[2] = 5, [4] = 9};  /* designated initializers (C99) */\n\n    size_t n = sizeof a / sizeof a[0];     /* element count */\n    printf(\"n=%zu sizeof(c)=%zu\\n\", n, sizeof c);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", b[i]);\n    printf(\"| %d | \", d[3]);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 6; i++) printf(\"%d \", e[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>n=5 sizeof(c)=12\n1 2 0 0 0 | 0 | 0 0 5 0 9 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>sizeof arr / sizeof arr[0]</code> gives the element count — but <strong>only</strong> where <code>arr</code> is a real array. Inside a function that received it as a parameter, <code>arr</code> is a pointer and the trick silently returns 2 (8 / 4).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layout-columns\"></i>Memory layout</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Value</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>30</td><td>40</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Offset (bytes)</td><td>0</td><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>12</td><td>16</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>a[i]</code> is defined as <code>*(a + i)</code>: start address plus <code>i × sizeof(int)</code>. That is why indexing is O(1) — and why an index past the end silently reads whatever lies next in memory.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>Passing arrays to functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n/* the array decays to a pointer: the size must travel separately */\nint max_of(const int arr[], size_t n) {\n    int best = arr[0];\n    for (size_t i = 1; i &lt; n; i++)\n        if (arr[i] &gt; best) best = arr[i];\n    return best;\n}\n\nvoid double_all(int *arr, size_t n) {     /* same as int arr[] */\n    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) arr[i] *= 2;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int v[] = {3, 9, 2, 7};\n    size_t n = sizeof v / sizeof v[0];\n    double_all(v, n);                      /* modifies the caller's array */\n    printf(\"max = %d, v[0] = %d\\n\", max_of(v, n), v[0]);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>max = 18, v[0] = 6</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-search\"></i>Common array algorithms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {4, -1, 7, 3, 7, 0};\n    int n = sizeof a / sizeof a[0];\n\n    int sum = 0, maxi = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        sum += a[i];\n        if (a[i] &gt; a[maxi]) maxi = i;\n    }\n    printf(\"sum=%d max=%d at %d\\n\", sum, a[maxi], maxi);\n\n    /* linear search */\n    int key = 3, pos = -1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) if (a[i] == key) { pos = i; break; }\n    printf(\"3 found at %d\\n\", pos);\n\n    /* rotate left by one */\n    int first = a[0];\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++) a[i] = a[i + 1];\n    a[n - 1] = first;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>sum=20 max=7 at 2\n3 found at 3\n-1 7 3 7 0 4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-horizontal\"></i>Variable-length arrays (C99)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 4;\n    int squares[n];                   /* size known only at run time: VLA on the stack */\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) squares[i] = i * i;\n    printf(\"%d %zu\\n\", squares[3], sizeof squares);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>9 16</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>VLAs live on the stack: a large or attacker-controlled size overflows the stack with no error reported. They became <em>optional</em> in C11, and the Linux kernel bans them. Prefer <code>malloc</code> for run-time sizes.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Out of bounds is undefined behaviour</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — caught by the sanitizer</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; i++)    /* off by one: touches a[5] */\n        sum += a[i];\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>UBSan</figcaption><pre><code>main.c:7:16: runtime error: index 5 out of bounds for type 'int[5]'</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Keep the length next to the pointer (<code>const int *a, size_t n</code>), and loop with <code>i &lt; n</code>.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Rely on a sentinel you never wrote, or on <code>sizeof</code> of a parameter.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't you assign one array to another with =?</summary><div><p>An array is not a modifiable lvalue: in most expressions its name decays to a pointer to its first element, so <code>a = b</code> would mean assigning to an address. Copy element by element or with <code>memcpy(a, b, sizeof a)</code>. (Wrapping an array in a <code>struct</code> makes it copyable by assignment.)</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is array decay?</summary><div><p>In almost every expression, an array of <code>T</code> is converted to a pointer to its first element (<code>T *</code>). The exceptions are <code>sizeof arr</code>, <code>&amp;arr</code> and a string literal used to initialise a <code>char</code> array. Decay is why functions cannot know an array's length.</p></div></details></section>"}